[font_size=22][b]Welcome to ePlugin Framework[/b][/font_size]

ePlugin takes care of the setup that C# editor plugins need. Plugins built on it ([i]ePlugins[/i]) tell the framework what they require, and it is installed for you when you enable them and removed again when you disable them.

[b]What it does for you[/b]
[ul]
[b]Automatic setup:[/b] NuGet packages, C# projects, autoloads and plugin files are added to your project when you enable an ePlugin, and removed when you disable it.
[b]Dependencies:[/b] plugins an ePlugin needs are enabled first, and their versions are checked.
[b]Updates:[/b] plugins with an update source, and ZIP packages in folders you choose, are checked for new versions. Updates are validated and backed up, and a failed update is rolled back.
[b]Licenses and welcome pages:[/b] a plugin can ask you to accept its license before it is enabled, and greet you once after it is installed, like this page.
[/ul]

[b]Where to start[/b]
Open the [b]ePlugin Manager[/b] with the ePlugin button at the right of the main toolbar, or from [b]Project > Tools > ePlugin Manager...[/b]. It lists all plugins of the project. You can enable and disable them, read their details and licenses, install updates or other versions, and retry work that failed.

[b]Good to know[/b]
[ul]
The editor is busy for a moment while an ePlugin is enabled or disabled, because the project is rebuilt.
Commit [code]addons/eplugin-state.json[/code] with your addons. It records the plugin versions, accepted licenses and shown welcome pages for your team. Do not commit [code]addons/eplugin-state.json.user[/code]; it only holds local recovery state.
[/ul]

Documentation and source: [url=https://github.com/enaweg/godot-epluginframework]github.com/enaweg/godot-epluginframework[/url]
